WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.240
You turn your head, and it does not feel like being shoved.

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.520
A push from outside moves your head too.

3
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.980
In alert monkeys, semicircular-canal afferents respond to head motion whether the

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.420
animal causes it or something else does.

5
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.100
They respond to both.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.720
So where does the difference arise?

7
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:26.433
The answer is central.

8
00:00:27.553 --> 00:00:33.193
Roy and Cullen recorded vestibular-only neurons in four alert macaques, in vivo.

9
00:00:34.253 --> 00:00:36.633
These are neurons of the vestibular nuclei.

10
00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:40.093
They respond to head motion, but not to eye movements.

11
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.533
The researchers varied conditions such as head restraint and neck torque, with

12
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:48.173
magnetic search coils tracking head and gaze.

13
00:00:49.173 --> 00:00:53.033
The afferents kept firing, so the cancellation is central.

14
00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:55.803
Now take made-up numbers.

15
00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:58.483
They are an illustration, not data.

16
00:00:59.123 --> 00:01:02.343
A passive turn peaks at 20 degrees per second.

17
00:01:02.803 --> 00:01:05.763
It evokes a modulation of 40 spikes per second.

18
00:01:06.703 --> 00:01:10.003
Divide the modulation by the peak speed to get sensitivity.

19
00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:17.083
Here it is 2.0. An active turn of the same size evokes only 4 spikes per second.

20
00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:22.603
Sensitivity is 0.2, so the response is 90 percent cancelled.

21
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.823
What lets the neuron cancel?

22
00:01:26.803 --> 00:01:32.083
In the neurons tested, cancelling the response to active motion required neck

23
00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:33.783
proprioceptive activation.

24
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:36.123
That is feedback from the neck.

25
00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:40.943
And it had to match the feedback expected from the motor command.

26
00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:44.063
The command sets what the neck should report.

27
00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:47.417
Remember, these numbers are made up.

28
00:01:48.497 --> 00:01:52.477
Now suppose neck feedback is made to mismatch the prediction.

29
00:01:53.277 --> 00:01:56.817
The sensitivity is expected to climb back toward 2.0.

30
00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:00.177
When the match failed, the cancellation failed.

31
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.730
Three limits matter.

32
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.830
The comparator circuit was not located.

33
00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.570
Not every vestibular neuron was shown to cancel.

34
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.970
And cancelling does not mean the periphery switched off, since the afferents kept

35
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:19.070
firing. The recordings show what these neurons did.

36
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.950
They do not show that these neurons alone produce the behavior.

37
00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.790
Cancellation is conditional, not automatic.

38
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:33.450
Keep this rule. In the neurons tested, the cancelling is central, and it held only

39
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.410
when neck feedback matched the expected feedback.
